---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import stats from "../../data/generated/stats.json";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology and data quality"
  description="Extraction, identity-resolution, historical-research, confidence, publication, and archival-review methods for Before OSS."
  eyebrow="Methodology and data quality"
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>An auditable method for incomplete evidence</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The project separates what the index prints, who a row may represent,
        what sources document, and what the evidence can establish about sequence.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="section">
    <div class="narrow prose">
      <h2>The source index</h2>
      <p>
        The starting source is the National Archives OSS personnel-file index for
        Record Group 226, Entry A1-224. It is an index to personnel files, not a
        roster of only commissioned officers, and it does not itself state a
        previous employer for each row.
      </p>
      <p>
        All 522 pages were processed. The pipeline preserved
        <strong> {Number(stats.source_rows).toLocaleString("en-US")} printed rows</strong>
        and linked them to
        <strong> {Number(stats.person_entities).toLocaleString("en-US")} cautious person entities</strong>.
        Repeated headers and page footers were excluded only after coordinate-level
        checks; raw fields remain immutable beside normalized search fields.
      </p>

      <h2>Extraction and visual validation</h2>
      <p>
        Embedded text was extracted twice: once with layout preservation and once
        as word coordinates. The two representations had to agree page by page.
        Pages 1–521 contain 46 rows each; page 522 contains 12. The first five,
        middle five, final five, and 50 deterministic random pages were rendered
        and inspected. All parser-warning pages are always selected for review.
      </p>

      <h2>Source rows are not people</h2>
      <p>
        Every printed line is a <span class="code-term">source_record</span>.
        A <span class="code-term">person_entity</span> may link one or more rows
        only when evidence supports that resolution. Exact normalized names alone
        never cause a merge. The automatic rule presently requires the same
        non-empty normalized service number as well as the same normalized name.
      </p>
      <p>
        A common name requires at least two corroborating identifiers beyond the
        name. Conflicting serials remain separate. Possible duplicate clusters are
        visible and retain a human-review requirement.
      </p>

      <h2>Three affiliation questions</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Immediate pre-OSS affiliation</strong>: the last organization,
          military assignment, institutional status, or other documented
          relationship directly before entry into OSS.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Last civilian employer before service</strong>: the last
          identifiable civilian employer before military service or an intervening
          wartime government assignment.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Other documented pre-OSS work</strong>: earlier work definitely
          before OSS but not demonstrated to be the immediate predecessor.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Identity, chronology, and confidence</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Controlled assessment</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Identity</td>
            <td>confirmed, high confidence, probable, ambiguous, conflicting, unresolved</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Temporal basis</td>
            <td>explicit immediate, strongly date-bounded, probable immediate, documented prewar, uncertain relation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Source quality</td>
            <td>A direct official through E discovery-only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Claim confidence</td>
            <td>confirmed, high, medium, low, unresolved, conflicting</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Confirmed and high claims may appear normally. Medium claims must remain
        visibly qualified. Low candidates stay private to review. Conflicting
        evidence is labeled and excluded from default aggregates.
      </p>

      <h2>Research completion</h2>
      <p>
        A query is not a completed biography. The minimum no-result protocol
        requires an official index-context check, a CIA or equivalent official
        check, meaningful exact-name OSS variants, employment or occupation
        searches, an applicable institutional/newspaper/obituary/directory check,
        recorded candidate rejections, and an archival-file recommendation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Until that protocol is completed, a profile says research has not yet been
        completed. After a completed protocol finds no reliable affiliation, it
        uses the narrower statement: “No reliable pre-OSS employer has yet been
        identified in the accessible sources reviewed.”
      </p>

      <h2>Archival review</h2>
      <p>
        Many indexed files are not digitized. Every unresolved record can flow to
        a box-and-location pull list. Failure to locate online evidence is treated
        as a reason to inspect the indexed personnel file, never as proof that the
        person had no prior employment.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy and corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Public data mask service numbers and omit private notes, credentials,
        sensitive identifiers, and low-confidence candidates. Each profile links
        to a prefilled correction form; proposed changes require a source and are
        imported as review decisions rather than edited into generated data.
      </p>

      <h2>NARA API notice</h2>
      <p>
        This product uses the National Archives Catalog API but is not endorsed or
        certified by the National Archives and Records Administration. API payloads
        are processed in memory and are not preserved; only minimal stable pointers
        and project-authored review notes may enter the research database.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
